{
 "id": "2022-lee-rooftop",
 "name": "Rooftop Vegetation Structure and Perception",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the original images: four computer-simulated views of the same concrete rooftop of a 15-storey commercial building in central Seoul, with no vegetation (control, 0% green), grass with a walking deck (G, 30%), grass and shrubs (GS, 50%) and grass, shrubs and trees (GST, 70%); buildings in the background are identical. Participants see the four images in random order, each for 90 s with a 30 s blank screen in between, and after each image rate it on the paper's four semantic-differential items on a 7-point scale: natural - artificial, open - closed, attractive - unattractive and comfortable - uncomfortable (stored so that 7 = natural / open / attractive / comfortable). Age and gender are asked at the end. Rebuilt from Lee, Kang, Lee & Lee (2022), Effects of Vegetation Structure on Psychological Restoration in an Urban Rooftop Space, International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health 20(1): 260, with the images cropped from its Figure 1 (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: control, G, GS, GST. Analysis as in the paper: one-way ANOVA with Scheffé post hoc tests across the four images for each SD item; the paper (15 young men) found GS and GST rated significantly more natural, attractive and comfortable than the bare concrete roof, while G did not differ significantly from it. Differences from the original study: (1) online, without the ECG and blood-pressure measures (HRV high frequency, LF/HF, systolic and diastolic pressure); (2) the Korean Profile of Mood States (K-POMS) is not included because the paper does not print its items; (3) the dummy image shown first in the lab is not included because the paper does not provide it; (4) the paper lists the SD pairs but not their left-right order or labels, so the page shows the negative pole on the left and the item wording is ours; (5) the paper's sample was 15 men aged about 24; anyone can take part here and the age and gender questions are ours; (6) the paper does not say whether the SD items were answered after each image or once at the end; they are asked after each image here; (7) the intro text is ours.",
